{"kind":"request","text":"Planner: review every API surface together for parity, consistency and what can be cut. Hugh asked for this on 2026-08-22; the ask is recorded in assert 2da0d79f.\n\nTHE SURFACES. The gs CLI, the MCP adapter, the resident HTTP endpoints, the public host package, and the web UI. They all express the same vocabulary -- requests, promises, reports, artifacts, verdicts, presence -- and they grew at different times under different requests, so they have drifted. This review is the first time anyone looks at all of them in one sitting.\n\nWHAT DRIFT LOOKS LIKE HERE, from things I have hit myself rather than imagined. gs inspect takes one event and refuses a list, while the MCP inspect takes an events array; I called both this week and only one of them worked the way I expected. Verdicts are readable from body.verdict, from body.status, or from text alone, in mixed case, so a reader has to know all three shapes. Some surfaces resolve a #N reference and some do not. gs status takes minutes locally and under a second through the resident, which means the CLI and the resident are not the same tool wearing two coats. Those are examples of the class, not the finding; check them at current main before repeating any of them.\n\nTHE THREE QUESTIONS, in the order that matters. Parity: what can you do on one surface that you cannot do on another, and is the gap deliberate? A missing capability is only a defect if the surface claims to be complete. Consistency: where the same operation exists twice, do the argument shapes, the defaults, the error text and the return shape agree? Disagreement is the expensive kind of drift, because it teaches a caller a rule that is false elsewhere. Simplification: what exists that nobody uses, what is expressible two ways for no reason, and what would be smaller if one surface simply called another?\n\nWHAT WOULD MAKE THIS WORTHLESS. A matrix of every operation against every surface with no judgement attached; anyone can generate that and it tells you nothing about what to fix. And a recommendation to unify surfaces that are deliberately different -- the CLI is for a person at a terminal, the MCP is for an agent, the resident is for speed, the UI is for a human reading; identical is not the goal, coherent is.\n\nDeliver findings, not a head. Rank them, and where something is worth doing, file a child request with its own conditions. A compatibility-breaking change needs a proposal for hugh, not a request.","body":{"conditions":"Review the gs CLI, the MCP adapter, the resident HTTP endpoints, the public host package and the web UI together, and report findings. (1) Build the operation-by-surface picture from the code at current main -- every command, tool, endpoint and exported entry point, named with its file -- but publish judgement, not the raw matrix. (2) Parity: list capabilities present on one surface and absent on another, and for each say whether the gap is deliberate and whether the surface claims completeness. (3) Consistency: for every operation that exists on more than one surface, compare argument shape, defaults, error text and return shape, and flag every disagreement that would teach a caller a false rule. Include the verdict-shape question: body.verdict, body.status and text-only all occur, and say what the single readable shape should be. (4) Simplification: identify surface area that is unused, expressible two ways for no reason, or that could be one surface calling another; say what removing it would cost. (5) Do not recommend making the surfaces identical; they have different audiences. Rank findings by what they cost a caller today. Deliver a report, file child requests for the work worth doing, and file a proposal for anything that breaks compatibility; this request is satisfied by the report, not by an implementation.","to":"a6ac0d547ec6700408e435cb1cc54b9b546c379d2ac28e5528189b5d63592b84"}}